amend(259,fate='EGAIV4 Theorem18.1.2, p.110, gives equivalence of étale schemes for a closed immersion with the same underlying space; its proof lifts standard smooth equations and glues by uniqueness. For a nilpotent ideal the finite subcategories are preserved. Stacks0BQB Lemma58.8.3 gives the exact finite-étale version. Both passages were opened.',refs=['EGAIV4','Stacks0BQB'],fate_date='EGAIV4 published1967; current counterpart checked2026')
amend(260,fate='Exact opened EGAIV4 counterparts: Proposition17.3.3(ii)–(iii), p.61 (composition/base change); Corollary17.6.2, p.71 (flat and unramified criterion); Proposition17.6.3, p.71 (noetherian completion criterion); Corollary17.7.3(ii), p.72 (faithfully flat descent, with local finite presentation or quasi-compact descent map); Proposition17.5.7, p.70 (reducedness). Opened Stacks0G1A covers the local standard-étale formulation. These are exact published matches, not historical attribution inferred from a modern reference.',refs=['EGAIV4','Stacks00U2','Stacks0257','Stacks0G1A'])
amend(261,statement='For a finite totally tamely ramified extension L/K of complete discretely valued fields, of degree e prime to the residue characteristic, there is a uniformizer α of L and u∈O_K^× with α^e=uπ_K, and L=K(α). No assumption μ_e⊂K is needed for this radical presentation. If μ_e⊂K, it also describes the cyclic Galois action. In the complex local model z=w^e a positive loop acts by w↦exp(2πi/e)w; that monodromy scalar is distinct from the global cycle-trace normalization (2πi)^−p.',
      fate='Classical tame ramification: choose a uniformizer β of L, lift the residue of β^e/π_K to u∈O_K^×, and extract an e-th root of the resulting principal unit by Hensel; computations.md §9 gives the proof. Opened étale/different sources supply the surrounding theory, and cote18 comparison endpoints were read. Roots of unity are required only for the indicated cyclic Galois interpretation, not for the radical presentation.',
      present_status='As of 2026-11-19: The complete totally tame radical presentation and the complex monodromy computation are established. The two normalization problems are distinct.')
